//! # Structured concurrency
//!
//! `scope` owns child tasks. A `fork` starts work, and scope exit waits for all
//! children. `await task` joins a named value-producing fork. If one child
//! traps, sibling structured tasks are cancelled and the trap escapes the
//! scope; lambda actors are independent and are not sibling-cancelled.
//!
//! ```hew
//! fn left_half() -> i64 { 20 }
//! fn right_half() -> i64 { 22 }
//!
//! actor Totals {
//!     receive fn combined() -> i64 {
//!         var total = 0;
//!         scope {
//!             fork left = left_half();
//!             fork right = right_half();
//!             let a = await left;
//!             let b = await right;
//!             total = a + b;
//!         }
//!         total
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let totals = spawn Totals;
//!     match await totals.combined() {
//!         .Ok(value) => println(f"{value}"),
//!         .Err(_) => println("ask failed"),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Two placement rules govern where this form is legal today.
//!
//! First, `scope { fork ... }` suspends, and every suspension point needs an
//! execution context to park. Every Hew function may suspend and none is
//! marked as suspending, so the rule is about the context a call runs on, not
//! about the function it sits in: an actor handler and a task body carry a
//! coroutine context, and a free function inherits its caller's. `fn main`
//! gains a context whose park and unpark are the main thread's parker; until
//! it does, a `fork` written directly in `fn main`, or in a free function
//! `main` calls, is refused with `E_LIMIT_MAIN_CONTEXT` rather than parked on
//! a contextless wait. Host the loop in an actor and park `main` on its ask,
//! or use `join {}` for a fan-out from `main`.
//!
//! Second, a statement in a `scope` body that is a bare call is read as a task
//! spawn, not as an ordinary call. `println(f"{a},{b}")` inside the scope is
//! therefore rejected as a spawn of `println` rather than executed — carry the
//! result out through a `var` assigned inside the scope and print after the
//! scope joins, as above.
//!
//! A value-producing `fork` currently takes no arguments; an argument-bearing
//! `fork name = f(args)` requires a unit-returning `f`. Value propagation out
//! of argument-bearing spawns is fail-closed rather than silently dropped.
//!
//! `join` is the actor-ask fan-out form. It issues every branch concurrently,
//! waits for all replies, and binds a tuple in source order. Two branches have
//! result shape `(R0, R1)`, not a list, map, or nested `Result`:
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Doubler {
//!     receive fn compute(value: i64) -> i64 { value * 2 }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let a = spawn Doubler;
//!     let b = spawn Doubler;
//!     let (first, second) = join {
//!         a.compute(10),
//!         b.compute(11),
//!     };
//!     assert_eq(first, 20);
//!     assert_eq(second, 22);
//!     println(f"{first},{second}");
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `scope` and `join` are distinct: scope manages task lifetime, while join
//! collects ordered actor replies. Cancellation is cooperative at suspension
//! points and is part of normal scope teardown, not an exception to the model.
